Let me set title first.', 'tool_calls': ToolCalls(tool_calls=[ToolCall(id='call_00_ykiJAQnELxMyvB7BgarA5385', name='set_title', args={'title': 'Cylinders and Spheres: Their Properties'})], tool_call_results=ToolCallResults(tool_call_results=[ToolCallResult(call_id='call_00_ykiJAQnELxMyvB7BgarA5385', name='set_title', value="Title set to 'Cylinders and Spheres: Their Properties'.", is_error=False)]))}, {'next_thought': "Let me write the section. I'll add explanation blocks and practice.", 'tool_calls': ToolCalls(tool_calls=[ToolCall(id='call_00_i4wC29xjLphdkpJYzEye4627', name='add_block', args={'block_type': 'explanation', 'text': 'Two of the most familiar three-dimensional figures around you are the cylinder and the sphere. A can of drink is a cylinder; a football or a ball of wool is a sphere. In this section you will learn to identify their properties — the faces, edges and vertices that each one has.

Start with the cylinder. Picture a solid tin upright in front of you, as in fig-01. A solid cylinder has two circular faces: one on top and one at the bottom. Joining them together around the outside is a single curved face that rolls around the whole body. So altogether a solid cylinder has:

- two circular faces and one curved face, making **three faces** altogether;
- **no vertex** — there is no sharp corner or point anywhere on it;
- **two curved edges**, the two circles where each circular face meets the curved face.

Now look at the sphere in fig-02. A sphere is a perfectly round figure, like a ball. Its properties are simpler still:

- it is **symmetrical from all directions** — however you turn it, it looks the same;
- it has **no edge and no vertex** — there are no straight lines, corners or points on it;
- it has **one curved surface** that covers the whole figure.

