[b0] (explanation)
The **perimeter** of a shape is the distance all the way round it. To find a perimeter you add up the lengths of every side. For shapes whose sides are equal or come in pairs, there are quick ways to write that addition, and for a circle the perimeter has its own name — the *circumference*. In this section you will find the perimeter of squares, rectangles, trapeziums, parallelograms, regular polygons and circles, and you will also work backwards from a given perimeter to find a missing side.

[b1] (worked_example)
**1. Perimeter of a square.** A square has four equal sides. If each side is called $L$, then

$$\text{Perimeter} = L + L + L + L = 4L$$

A square of side 15cm (fig-05) has perimeter

$$4 \times 15 = 60\text{cm}.$$

[b2] (worked_example)
**2. Perimeter of a rectangle.** A rectangle has two equal long sides (length $L$) and two equal short sides (breadth $b$). Going round the shape:

$$\text{Perimeter} = L + b + L + b = 2L + 2b = 2(L + b)$$

Find the perimeter of a rectangle of length 10cm and breadth 7cm (fig-06):

$$P = 2(10 + 7) = 2(17) = 34\text{cm}.$$

**3. Working backwards to find a missing side.** Suppose the perimeter of a rectangle is 60cm and its length is 17cm. We want the breadth $b$.

$$60 = 17 + b + 17 + b = 2(17) + 2b = 34 + 2b$$

Collect like terms:

$$60 - 34 = 2b \quad\Rightarrow\quad 26 = 2b$$

Divide both sides by 2:

$$b = 13\text{cm}.$$

[b3] (worked_example)
**4. Perimeter of a trapezium.** A trapezium has four sides, all possibly different, lettered $a$, $b$, $c$ and $d$ going round. Its perimeter is simply

$$\text{Perimeter} = a + b + c + d.$$

For the trapezium in fig-08 with sides 11cm, 9cm, 5cm and 4cm:

$$P = 11 + 9 + 5 + 4 = 29\text{cm}.$$

**5. Perimeter of a parallelogram.** A parallelogram has two pairs of equal sides: the two horizontal ones are each $a$, and the two slanted ones are each $b$. So

$$\text{Perimeter} = a + a + b + b = 2a + 2b = 2(a + b).$$

For the parallelogram in fig-09, $a = 9\text{cm}$ and $b = 5\text{cm}$:

$$P = 2(9 + 5) = 2(14) = 28\text{cm}.$$

[b4] (worked_example)
**6. Perimeter of a regular polygon.** A regular polygon has all its sides equal, so its perimeter is (number of sides) × (length of one side). A regular hexagon has 6 equal sides. The hexagon in fig-10 has sides of 5.2cm:

$$P = 6 \times 5.2 = 31.2\text{cm}.$$

[b5] (worked_example)
**7. Perimeter (circumference) of a circle.** The perimeter of a circle is called its *circumference*:

$$C = 2\pi r \quad\text{or}\quad C = \pi d$$

where $r$ is the radius and $d = 2r$ is the diameter.

For a circle of radius 7cm (fig-11), using $\pi = \frac{22}{7}$:

$$C = 2 \times \frac{22}{7} \times 7 = 2 \times 22 = 44\text{cm}.$$

**8. A rope round a cylinder.** If a rope is wound round a cylinder, one turn round it is one circumference. A rope wound 50 times round a cylinder of radius 25cm covers a total length of $50 \times$ circumference. With $\pi = 3.14$:

$$\text{One turn} = 2 \times 3.14 \times 25 = 157\text{cm}$$

$$\text{Total} = 157 \times 50 = 7850\text{cm}.$$

**9. Finding the radius from the circumference.** If the circumference of a circle is 440cm, find its radius using $\pi = \frac{22}{7}$:

$$440 = 2 \times \frac{22}{7} \times r = \frac{44r}{7}$$

Cross multiply:

$$440 \times 7 = 44r \quad\Rightarrow\quad 3080 = 44r$$

Divide both sides by 44:

$$r = \frac{3080}{44} = 70\text{cm}.$$

[b6] (worked_example)
**10. A shape made of straight sides and an arc.** Some shapes combine straight sides with part of a circle. The shape in fig-14 has two straight sides of 4.9cm and a quarter of a circle closing them. The curved part is $\frac14$ of a full circumference of radius 4.9cm:

$$\frac14 \times 2\pi r = \frac14 \times 2 \times \frac{22}{7} \times 4.9 = \frac{11}{7} \times 4.9 = 7.7\text{cm}.$$

So the whole perimeter is the two straight sides plus the curved part:

$$P = 4.9 + 4.9 + 7.7 = 17.5\text{cm}.$$

