[b0] (explanation)
Two lines are parallel when they run side by side and never meet, however far you extend them. In this section you will construct, through a given point P, a line that is parallel to a given line AB — using only your set square and ruler.

You will need your mathematical set. Take your set square and lay it on your page with one of its straight edges lying exactly along the line AB, so the right angle sits at the left end of that edge and its other short edge runs down away from AB (fig-01).

Now take your ruler and lay it along the other edge of the set square — the sloping edge. If you are right-handed, it is easier to slide the set square away from you, so rest the ruler along the left-hand sloping edge. Hold the ruler firmly still on the page (fig-03). It does not matter which way up the set square faces or how the ruler leans; what matters is that one edge of the set square lies along AB and the ruler rests firmly against another edge.

Keeping the ruler held firmly in place, slide the set square along the ruler towards P. Slide it until the edge that was lying on AB reaches P. Then, keeping the set square pressed against the ruler, draw a line along that edge of the set square through P (fig-05). Because you slid the set square along a fixed ruler without turning it, the edge has kept its direction — so the line you draw through P is parallel to AB.

This is why the slide works: sliding the set square along the ruler moves it without turning it. The edge that first lay along AB keeps exactly the same direction as it slides, so the edge passing through P is parallel to AB.

[b1] (worked_example)
Let's work through the construction of a line through P parallel to AB, step by step.

Start with line AB and the point P marked above it (fig-01).

Step 1 — Place the set square so that one edge lies exactly along AB, with the right angle at the left end of that edge. The other short edge runs down away from the line.

Step 2 — Lay the ruler along the sloping edge of the set square (use the left-hand edge if you are right-handed). Hold the ruler firmly so it cannot move (fig-03).

Step 3 — Keeping the ruler still, slide the set square along the ruler towards P. Watch the edge that was lying on AB. Stop the moment that edge reaches P.

Step 4 — With the set square pressed against the ruler, draw a line along that edge through P. This line passes through P and is parallel to AB (fig-05).

The whole slide is possible because the ruler stays fixed: the set square moves along it without turning, so the edge that started on AB keeps its direction all the way to P. The line you draw is therefore parallel to AB.

