[b0] (explanation)
Perimeter is the distance round a shape. To find it, you add up the lengths of all the sides that make the boundary of the shape. For a circle, the perimeter has its own special name — the <b>circumference</b> — because there are no straight sides to add. As you work through this section you will find the perimeter of squares, rectangles, trapeziums, parallelograms, regular polygons and circles, and you will also work backwards to find a missing side when the perimeter is already known.

[b1] (worked_example)
A square has four equal sides, so its perimeter is four times one side, $4L$.

<b>Example 1.</b> Calculate the perimeter of a square of side 15cm.

Perimeter $= L + L + L + L = 4L = 4(15) = 60\mathrm{cm}$.

[b2] (worked_example)
A rectangle has two equal long sides (length $L$) and two equal short sides (breadth $b$), so its perimeter is $2L + 2b$.

<b>Example 2.</b> Find the perimeter of a rectangle whose length is 10cm and breadth 7cm.

Perimeter $= L + b + L + b = 2L + 2b = 2(10) + 2(7) = 20 + 14 = 34\mathrm{cm}$.

<b>Example 3.</b> If the perimeter of a rectangle is 60cm and its length is 17cm, calculate its breadth.

$P = 2L + 2b$, so $60 = 2(17) + 2b = 34 + 2b$.

Collect like terms: $60 - 34 = 2b$, so $2b = 26\mathrm{cm}$.

Divide both sides by 2: $b = 13\mathrm{cm}$.

[b3] (worked_example)
A trapezium has four sides that are usually all different, so its perimeter is simply the sum of all four: $a + b + c + d$.

<b>Example 4.</b> A trapezium has sides of 11cm, 9cm, 5cm and 4cm. Find its perimeter.

Perimeter $= 11 + 9 + 5 + 4 = 29\mathrm{cm}$.

[b4] (worked_example)
A parallelogram has two pairs of equal opposite sides. Let the base be $a$ and the slanted side be $b$; then the perimeter is $2a + 2b = 2(a + b)$.

<b>Example 5.</b> Find the perimeter of a parallelogram whose base is 9cm and slanted side is 5cm.

Perimeter $= 2(a + b) = 2(9 + 5) = 2(14) = 28\mathrm{cm}$.

[b5] (worked_example)
A <b>regular</b> polygon has all its sides equal. So for any regular polygon, the perimeter is the number of sides multiplied by the length of one side. A hexagon has 6 sides.

<b>Example 6.</b> Find the perimeter of a regular hexagon of side 5.2cm.

Perimeter $= 6 \times 5.2 = 31.2\mathrm{cm}$.

[b6] (worked_example)
The perimeter of a circle is called its <b>circumference</b>. If the radius is $r$, then
$$\text{Circumference} = 2\pi r = \pi d,$$
where $d = 2r$ is the diameter.

<b>Example 7.</b> Calculate the circumference of a circle of radius 7cm, using $\pi = \frac{22}{7}$.

Using $2\pi r$: $\text{Circumference} = 2 \times \frac{22}{7} \times 7 = 2 \times 22 = 44\mathrm{cm}$.

Using $\pi d$: $d = 2 \times 7 = 14\mathrm{cm}$, so $\text{Circumference} = \frac{22}{7} \times 14 = 22 \times 2 = 44\mathrm{cm}$.

<b>Example 8.</b> A rope is wound 50 times round a cylinder of radius 25cm. How long is the rope? ($\pi = 3.14$)

Each turn of the rope covers one circumference, so first find the circumference.

Circumference $= 2\pi r = 2 \times 3.14 \times 25 = 157\mathrm{cm}$.

Total length $= 157 \times 50 = 7850\mathrm{cm}$.

<b>Example 9.</b> The circumference of a circle is 440cm. Find its radius. ($\pi = \frac{22}{7}$)

Circumference $= 2\pi r$, so $440 = 2 \times \frac{22}{7} \times r = \frac{44r}{7}$.

Cross-multiply: $440 \times 7 = 44r$, so $3080 = 44r$.

Divide both sides by 44: $r = \frac{3080}{44} = 70\mathrm{cm}$.

[b7] (worked_example)
Some shapes are made of straight sides and a curved part. A <b>quarter circle</b> has two straight sides and one curved part that is one quarter of a full circumference. Add all three parts to get the perimeter.

<b>Example 10.</b> Calculate the perimeter of a quarter circle whose two straight sides are each 4.9cm, using $\pi = \frac{22}{7}$.

Length of the curved part $= \frac{1}{4} \times 2\pi r = \frac{1}{4} \times 2 \times \frac{22}{7} \times 4.9 = \frac{11}{7} \times 4.9$.

Since $4.9 = \frac{49}{10}$, $\frac{11}{7} \times \frac{49}{10} = \frac{11 \times 7}{10} = 7.7\mathrm{cm}$.

Perimeter of the shape $= 4.9 + 4.9 + 7.7 = 17.5\mathrm{cm}$.

