Block 1 (explanation): Introduction to plane shapes.

"Plane shapes are also known as two-dimensional (2-D) shapes. They are flat shapes that lie on a plane. The plane shapes you will meet include the triangle, the square, the rectangle, the parallelogram, the trapezium, the circle, and the other polygons. In this section you will learn to identify the different types of plane shapes and state their properties, similarities and differences."

Block 2 (explanation): Triangles.

"Triangles are plane shapes with three straight sides and three angles. They come in three types, shown in fig-01.

- An isosceles triangle has two equal (adjacent) sides, marked with a single tick on each slanted side. Its base angles are equal, and it has one line of symmetry.
- A scalene triangle has no equal sides — the sides are marked with one, two and three ticks. No angle is equal to another, and it has no line of symmetry.
- An equilateral triangle has all three sides equal (each marked with a single tick). All its angles are equal, each 60°, and it has three lines of symmetry.

Compare them: the isosceles and equilateral triangles both have at least one pair of equal sides and at least one line of symmetry, while the scalene triangle has none. The equilateral triangle is the most special — all its sides and all its angles are equal."

Block 3 (explanation): Squares and rectangles.

"Squares and rectangles are both four-sided plane shapes whose four angles are all right angles (90°). They are shown in fig-02.

A square has all four sides equal (each marked with a single tick), two pairs of parallel sides, four lines of symmetry, and its diagonals bisect each other at a right angle.

A rectangle has two opposite sides equal — the two longer sides are marked with a single tick and the two shorter sides with a double tick. It has two pairs of parallel sides, two lines of symmetry, and its diagonals bisect each other but not at a right angle.

Similarity: both have four right angles and two pairs of parallel sides. Difference: all four sides of a square are equal, whereas only opposite sides of a rectangle are equal; a square has four lines of symmetry but a rectangle has two."

Block 4 (explanation): Parallelogram and trapezium.

"A parallelogram and a trapezium are both four-sided shapes with parallel sides, shown in fig-03.

A parallelogram has two pairs of opposite sides equal and parallel (the horizontal sides carry a single arrow, the slanted sides a double arrow). Its opposite angles are equal, its diagonals divide it into two equal triangles, and it has no line of symmetry.

A trapezium has only one pair of parallel sides (marked with a single arrow). Its opposite angles are not equal, its diagonal divides it into two unequal triangles, and it has no line of symmetry.

Similarity: both are quadrilaterals with at least one pair of parallel sides and neither has a line of symmetry. Difference: the parallelogram has two pairs of parallel sides and equal opposite sides and angles, while the trapezium has only one pair of parallel sides."

