Title: "Constructing Parallel Lines with a Set Square and Ruler"

Explanation block: 

"Two lines are parallel when they stay the same distance apart all the way along and never meet, however far you extend them. You can construct a line through a given point P that is parallel to a given line AB using just a set square and a ruler.

Here is the method, in three steps:

1. Place the set square so that one of its edges lies accurately along line AB.
2. Put the ruler against one of the other edges of the set square — say the left-hand edge if you are right-handed. Hold the ruler firmly so it does not move.
3. Keeping the ruler held still, slide the set square along the ruler towards P. Stop when the edge that was lying on AB reaches P. Draw a line along this edge through P. This new line is parallel to AB."

