[b0] (explanation)
Plane shapes are also called two-dimensional (2-D) shapes. They are flat shapes, and they include triangles, rectangles, squares, parallelograms, trapeziums, circles and other polygons.

Let us meet each family and learn the properties that make each one special.

[b1] (explanation)
<b>Triangles</b> (fig-01) — a triangle is a closed shape with three straight sides. There are three kinds.

An <b>isosceles</b> triangle has two equal sides (they are the two slanted sides) and two equal angles (the base angles). It has one line of symmetry.

A <b>scalene</b> triangle has no equal sides and no equal angles. It has no line of symmetry.

An <b>equilateral</b> triangle has all three sides equal and all three angles equal, each 60°. It has three lines of symmetry.

[b2] (explanation)
<b>Squares and rectangles</b> (fig-02) are four-sided shapes whose four angles are all right angles (90° each).

A <b>square</b> has all four sides equal, two pairs of parallel sides, four lines of symmetry, and its diagonals bisect it at right angles.

A <b>rectangle</b> has two pairs of opposite sides equal (two longer sides and two shorter sides), two pairs of parallel sides, two lines of symmetry, and its diagonals bisect it but not at right angles.

[b3] (explanation)
<b>Parallelogram and trapezium</b> (fig-03).

A <b>parallelogram</b> has two pairs of opposite sides equal and parallel, its opposite angles are equal, it has no line of symmetry, and its diagonals divide it into two equal triangles.

A <b>trapezium</b> has only one pair of parallel sides, its opposite angles are not equal, it has no line of symmetry, and its diagonal divides it into two unequal triangles.

[b4] (explanation)
<b>Polygons</b> — a polygon is a closed shape that has three or more straight sides. Every polygon can be divided into triangles by drawing diagonals from one vertex to all the non-adjacent vertices. The number of triangles is always the number of sides minus 2.

Look at how the names, the number of sides, and the number of triangles fit together:

| Name of polygon | Number of sides | Number of triangles (n − 2) |
|---|---|---|
| Triangle | 3 | 1 |
| Quadrilateral | 4 | 2 |
| Pentagon | 5 | 3 |
| Hexagon | 6 | 4 |
| Heptagon | 7 | 5 |
| Octagon | 8 | 6 |
| Nonagon | 9 | 7 |
| Decagon | 10 | 8 |
| Hendecagon (or undecagon) | 11 | 9 |
| Dodecagon | 12 | 10 |

Notice the pattern: a quadrilateral has 2 triangles (4 − 2), a pentagon has 3 triangles (5 − 2), and so on.

