Title: "Drawing a Line Parallel to Another"

Block 1 (explanation):
"Two lines are parallel when they run in the same direction and stay the same distance apart all the way along — no matter how far you extend them, they never meet. You will draw a line through a given point P that is parallel to a given line AB, using only your set square and ruler.

Here is the method, in three steps.

First, place one edge of your set square exactly along the line AB (fig-01). The right angle of the set square should sit on the line, with one leg lying along AB.

Second, rest your ruler against another edge of the set square and hold it firmly (fig-02, fig-03). If you are right-handed, place the ruler along the set square's left-hand edge and hold it with your left hand. It does not matter which edge the ruler rests against — the sloping one or the upright one — and it does not matter which way round the set square faces. Any set square works, so long as one edge lies on AB and the ruler rests against a second edge.

Third, keep the ruler perfectly still and slide the set square along it towards P (fig-04, fig-05). Because the ruler is held firmly, the set square slides without turning, so the edge that lay on AB keeps pointing the same way as it did at the start. Stop when that edge reaches P, and draw a line along it through P. Since the edge never turned, this new line runs in the same direction as AB — it is parallel to AB."

Block 2 (worked example):
"Example: Construct a line through P that is parallel to AB.
1. Put one edge of the set square exactly on AB, right angle on the line.
2. Rest the ruler against another edge of the set square and hold it firmly (left hand if you are right-handed).
3. Slide the set square along the ruler until the edge that was on AB reaches P.
4. Draw the line through P along that edge.
The line you have drawn is parallel to AB. You can check by sliding the set square back down to AB again — the two lines stay the same distance apart all the way along."

