[b0] (explanation)
Two lines are parallel when they never meet, however far you extend them — they run in the same direction, side by side. You are going to draw a line through a given point <i>P</i> so that it is parallel to a given line <i>AB</i>. To do this you need two things from your mathematical set: a set square and a ruler.

[b1] (explanation)
The whole trick is to let the set square carry the direction of <i>AB</i> up to <i>P</i>. A set square is a flat triangle with a right angle at one corner. If you slide a set square along a held ruler, it does not turn — it keeps pointing the same way. So if one edge of the set square lies along <i>AB</i>, and you slide it straight up the ruler until that edge reaches <i>P</i>, the edge still points the same way it did when it lay on <i>AB</i>. A line drawn along that edge through <i>P</i> therefore runs in the same direction as <i>AB</i> — it is parallel to <i>AB</i> (fig-05).

[b2] (explanation)
Here is the construction, step by step, for drawing a line through <i>P</i> parallel to <i>AB</i>.

<strong>Step (a) — put the set square on <i>AB</i>.</strong> Place the set square so that one of its edges lies exactly along <i>AB</i>. The right angle sits at one end of that edge (fig-01).

[b3] (explanation)
<strong>Step (b) — rest the ruler against another edge.</strong> Hold the ruler firmly along a different edge of the set square — if you are right handed, use the left-hand edge. This ruler is only a guide: it must not move while you slide. It does not matter how the ruler is tilted, so long as it rests against one of the set square's edges (fig-03). The right angle marked at the corner belongs to the set square, between the edge on <i>AB</i> and the edge running down from it.

[b4] (explanation)
<strong>Step (c) — slide the set square up to <i>P</i> and draw.</strong> Keep the ruler still. Slide the set square along the ruler towards <i>P</i>, and stop when the edge that was on <i>AB</i> reaches <i>P</i>. Draw a line along that edge through <i>P</i>. Because the slide moved the set square without turning it, this edge points the same way as it did on <i>AB</i>, so the line you have drawn is parallel to <i>AB</i> (fig-04, fig-05).

[p0] (practice)
Draw a line <i>CD</i> and mark a point <i>Q</i> above it. Construct a line through <i>Q</i> that is parallel to <i>CD</i>. Describe each step you take.
answer: Place one edge of a set square exactly along <i>CD</i>; rest the ruler firmly against another edge of the set square and hold it still; slide the set square along the ruler until the edge that was on <i>CD</i> reaches <i>Q</i>; draw a line along that edge through <i>Q</i>. That line is parallel to <i>CD</i>.

[p1] (practice)
