Title: "Perimeter of Plane Shapes"

Block 1 (explanation):
"Perimeter is the distance all the way round the outside of a shape. To find it, add up the lengths of every side. Different shapes have their own quick ways to do this, and you can always fall back on adding all the sides."

Then introduce. Let me do worked examples.

Worked example 1 - square:
"A square has four equal sides. Let each side be $L$. Its perimeter is
$P = L + L + L + L = 4L$.
If a square has side 15cm, then $P = 4 \times 15 = 60\text{cm}$."

Worked example 2 - rectangle:
"A rectangle has two long sides (length $L$) and two short sides (breadth $b$). Its perimeter is
$P = L + b + L + b = 2L + 2b = 2(L+b)$.
For a rectangle of length 10cm and breadth 7cm, $P = 2(10+7) = 2(17) = 34\text{cm}$."

Worked example 3 - rectangle missing breadth:
"Suppose a rectangle has perimeter 60cm and length 17cm. Write the perimeter formula and put in what you know:
$60 = 2(17 + b)$
$60 = 34 + 2b$
$60 - 34 = 2b$
$26 = 2b$
$b = 13\text{cm}$.
The breadth is 13cm."

Worked example 4 - trapezium:
"A trapezium has four sides. To find its perimeter, simply add the four lengths. For a trapezium with sides 11cm, 9cm, 5cm and 4cm:
$P = 11 + 9 + 5 + 4 = 29\text{cm}$."

Worked example 5 - parallelogram:
"A parallelogram has two pairs of equal sides: the two bases are $a$ and the two slanted sides are $b$. Its perimeter is
$P = a + a + b + b = 2a + 2b = 2(a+b)$.
For a parallelogram with base 9cm and slant side 5cm: $P = 2(9+5) = 2(14) = 28\text{cm}$."

Worked example 6 - regular hexagon:
"A regular polygon has all its sides equal and all its angles equal. A regular hexagon has 6 equal sides, so its perimeter is $6 \times \text{side}$.
For a regular hexagon of side 5.2cm: $P = 6 \times 5.2 = 31.2\text{cm}$."

Worked example 7 - circle:
"The perimeter of a circle is called its circumference. With radius $r$ (or diameter $d = 2r$),
$C = 2\pi r = \pi d$.
For a circle of radius 7cm, using $\pi = \frac{22}{7}$:
$C = 2 \times \frac{22}{7} \times 7 = 2 \times 22 = 44\text{cm}$.
(Using the diameter: $d = 14$cm, so $C = \frac{22}{7} \times 14 = 44\text{cm}$.)"

Worked example 8 - circle application (rope):
"Circumference is a real length, so it can be used in problems. If a rope is wound 50 times round a cylinder of radius 25cm, each turn is one circumference:
$C = 2 \times 3.14 \times 25 = 157\text{cm}$.
Fifty turns: $157 \times 50 = 7850\text{cm}$."

Worked example 9 - find radius:
"When you are given the circumference, you can work back to the radius. The circumference of a circle is 440cm. Find the radius, using $\pi = \frac{22}{7}$.
$C = 2\pi r$
$440 = 2 \times \frac{22}{7} \times r = \frac{44r}{7}$
$440 \times 7 = 44r$
$3080 = 44r$
$r = \frac{3080}{44} = 70\text{cm}$."

Worked example 10 - quarter circle:
"A quarter circle has two straight sides and a curved arc. The arc is one quarter of the full circumference.
For a quarter circle with straight sides 4.9cm:
$\text{arc} = \frac{1}{4} \times 2\pi r = \frac{1}{4} \times 2 \times \frac{22}{7} \times 4.9 = 7.7\text{cm}$.
Perimeter $= 4.9 + 4.9 + 7.7 = 17.5\text{cm}$."

